column389:
25/Apr/19 22:23;willshen;[~karanmehta93], getting close, but I am not quite sure how the iterators are managed...
When we reach the limit, we're closing the PeekingResultIterator in org.apache.phoenix.iterate.BaseResultIterators#close:
{code}
// Wait for already started tasks to complete as we can't interrupt them without
            // leaving our HConnection in a funky state.
            for (Future<PeekingResultIterator> future : futuresToClose) {
                try {
                    PeekingResultIterator iterator = future.get();
                    iterator.close();
                } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                    Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
                    throw new RuntimeException(e);
                } catch (ExecutionException e) {
                    logger.info("Failed to execute task during cancel", e);
                    continue;
                }
            }
{code}

When the limit is small, the iterator has scanMetricsUpdated to be true by this point, and will close without attempting to get scan metric. However, when the limit is large, the PeekingResultIterator will have scanMetricsUpdated to be false, and when we attempt to get scan metric on close, the NPE is thrown. This smells like there is a bug somewhere... but I am not too sure where to look.

column391:
30/Apr/19 01:29;willshen;[~tdsilva], I am able to reproduce locally with a sample program running against test dataset size of 1000000 rows generated using Pherf (sorry, not sure where to add an actual test because I've yet to nail down the exact condition):

{code}
package test;

import java.sql.*;
import java.util.Properties;

import org.apache.phoenix.query.QueryServices;

public class NPEExample {
    public static void main(final String[] args) throws SQLException {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.setProperty(QueryServices.COLLECT_REQUEST_LEVEL_METRICS, "true");
        Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:phoenix:localhost:2181", props);
        PreparedStatement stmt = conn.prepareStatement("SELECT * FROM PHERF.KEYWORDS_NOIDX LIMIT 400000");
        ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery();

        try {
            while (rs.next()) {
                rs.getObject(1);
            }
        } finally {
            rs.close();
            stmt.close();
            conn.close();
        }
    }
}
{code}

