lihalite commented on pull request #3477: ARROW-4213: [Flight] Fix incompatibilities between C++ and Java
URL: https://github.com/apache/arrow/pull/3477#discussion_r251831688



 ##########
 File path: cpp/src/arrow/flight/server.cc
 ##########
 @@ -111,7 +115,11 @@ class SerializationTraits<IpcPayload> {
     }

     // 2 bytes for body tag
-    total_size += 2 + WireFormatLite::LengthDelimitedSize(static_cast<size_t>(body_size));
+    // Only written when there are body buffers
+    if (msg.body_length > 0) {
+      total_size +=
+          2 + WireFormatLite::LengthDelimitedSize(static_cast<size_t>(body_size));
+    }

 Review comment:
   `ArrowMessage.asSchema` will fail if any body buffers are present, even if the buffer is 0-sized. Since otherwise C++ was always writing an (empty) body buffer, Java would always fail to decode the schema from a C++ client. IMO, it was cleaner to not send the buffer if empty on the C++ side, rather than detect whether the buffer was "real" on the Java side. Alternatively, Java could ignore the presence of body buffers.

